= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <bounded linear operator> is compact when the closure of the image of its <unit ball> is compact. Equivalently every bounded sequence has a subsequence whose images converge; on a Banach target it also suffices that the image of its <unit ball> be <totally bounded>.

For a <finite-rank operator> that image is bounded in a finite-dimensional subspace, whose closed bounded sets are compact. Thus **every <finite-rank operator> is compact**.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Given a bounded sequence, first pass to a subsequence on which the images under $T_1$ converge. From that subsequence extract another on which the images under $T_2$ converge. Both image sequences, and hence their sum, converge on the latter subsequence. Thus **$T_1+T_2$ is compact**.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Extract a subsequence whose $T$ images converge, and apply continuity of the bounded operator $S$ to those images. This proves **$ST$ is compact**. The reverse composition $TS$ is compact as well, since $S$ sends a bounded sequence to a bounded sequence to which compactness of $T$ applies.

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Suppose $T_n$ are compact and $\|T_n-T\|\to0$. For any $\varepsilon>0$ take $n$ with $\|T_n-T\|<\varepsilon$. A finite $\varepsilon$-net of $T_n$ of the <unit ball> is then a finite $2\varepsilon$-net of its $T$ image. That image is <totally bounded>, so $T$ is compact by the stated criterion. Hence **$\mathcal K(X)$ is <norm> closed in $B(X)$**.

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**Compact.** Let $P_N$ keep the first $N$ coordinates. The operator $P_NT$ has finite rank and $\|T-P_NT\|\leq1/(N+1)$, since the remaining diagonal coefficients are at most that number. <Norm>-closedness of <compact operators> proves the claim.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**Not compact.** For coordinate unit vectors $e_j$ with $j\geq2$, the images are $e_{j-1}$. Distinct such images have $\ell^1$ distance two, so no image subsequence converges.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**Compact.** This operator is $BD$, where $D$ is the compact diagonal operator in (a) and $B$ is the bounded backward shift in (b). The composition property applies. Equivalently its tails have the same vanishing-coefficient finite-rank approximation.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**Compact.** Its column indexed by $m$ has entries $n!/m!$ for $1\leq n<m$, and zero otherwise. The sum of that column is $c_m=(1!+\cdots+(m-1)!)/m!\leq2/m$ for $m\geq2$, by the elementary induction $1!+\cdots+k!\leq2k!$. For operators from $\ell^1$ the supremum of the absolute column sums is the <operator norm>: one inequality is the <triangle inequality>, and coordinate unit vectors give the reverse. Keeping just the first $N$ input columns is finite rank and its remainder <norm> is at most $\sup_{m>N}c_m\leq2/(N+1)$. Hence it converges to $T$ in <norm>, proving compactness.

